\f1\fs22  \f0\fs24\par
              In english grammar, there are14 puntuation marks and they are hyphen,dash,colon, semicolon,comma, exclamation  mark,period,parentheses,brackets,braces, apostrphe,quatation marks, and ellipsis.Rightful usage can help writting used to communicate easier to read and more appealing. \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b\f2\lang1033 The period (.)   : \b0 this\b  \b0 is placed at the end of a sentence (in the indicative\~mood) that makes a declaration statement thought to be complete and after many abbreviations. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 As a sentence ender    : \i linda and sack went to the mall.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}As an abbreviation      : \i Dr. Maxwell was born in  2008.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b Question mark(?)   : \b0 to indicate a direct question when placed at the end of a sentence. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i When did linda arrive?\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100  \b Exclamation point (!)  : \b0 is used when a person wants to express a sudden outcry or add emphasis. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 Within dialogues    : \i "Holy cow!" screamed Jane.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}To emphasize a point     : \i My mother-in-law's rants make me furious!\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100  \b Comma    :  \b0 it is used to show a separation of ideas or elements within a grammatical structure or a sentence.It is also used in numbers, dates, and letter writing after the salutation and closing. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 Direct address: \i Thanks for all your help, he said.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Separation of two complete sentences: \i We went to the cinema, and then we went out for lunch.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Separating lists or elements within sentences: \i Suzi wanted the brown, green, and red dress.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100  \b Semicolon (;)\b0    :   is used to connect independent clauses. It indicates a closer relationship between the clauses than a period would show. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i Caleb was hurt; he knew she only said it to disturb him.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100  \b Colon (:)   :   \b0 has three main uses. The first is after a word introducing a quotation, an explanation, an example, or a series. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i He was planning to study four subjects: politics, philosophy, sociology, and economics.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100 The second is between independent clauses when the second explains the first, similar to a semicolon: \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i I didn't have time to get changed: I was already late.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100 The third use of a colon is for emphasis: \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i There was one thing she loved more than any other: her dog.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100 A colon also has non-grammatical uses in time, ratio, business correspondence and references. \par
\par
\b Dash(-)     \b0 :  is used to separate words into statements. There are two common types of dashes: en dash and em dash. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 En dash: Twice as long as a hyphen, the en dash is a symbol (--) that is used in writing or printing to indicate a range, connections or differentiations, such as 1880-1945 or Princeton-New York trains. \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Em dash: Longer than the en dash, the em dash can be used in place of a comma, parenthesis, or colon to enhance readability or emphasize the conclusion of a sentence. For example, \i She gave him her answer --- No!\line\i0 Whether you put spaces around the em dash or not is a style choice. Just be consistent. \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b Hyphen(-)     :   \b0 It is used to join two or more words together into a compound term and is not separated by spaces. For example, part-time, back-to-back, well-known. \par
\b Brackets([])     :   \b0  are the squared off notations ([]) used for technical explanations or to clarify meaning. If you remove the information in the brackets, the sentence will still make sense. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i He [Mr. Jones] was the last person seen at the house.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b Braces (\{\})    \b0 :   they are used to contain two or more lines of text or listed items to show that they are considered as a unit. They are not commonplace in most writing but can be seen in computer programming to show what should be contained within the same lines. They can also be used in mathematical expressions. For example, \i 2\{2+[25+3]\}=x.\i0  \par
\b Parentheses ( () )   \b0 :  they are curved notations used to contain further thoughts or qualifying remarks. However, parentheses can be replaced by commas without changing the meaning in most cases. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i John and Jane (who were actually half brother and sister) both have red hair.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100  \b Apostrophe (')  \b0 :   is used to indicate the omission of a letter or letters from a word, the possessive case, or the plurals of lowercase letters. Examples of the apostrophe in use include: \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 Omission of letters from a word:     \i I've seen that movie several times. She wasn't the only one who knew the answer.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Possessive case:   \i Sara's dog bit the neighbor.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Plural for lowercase letters:    \i Six people were told to mind their p's and q's.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b Question Mark (" ")   \b0 :  They are a pair of punctuation marks used primarily to mark the beginning and end of a passage attributed to another and repeated word for word. They are also used to indicate meanings and to indicate the unusual or dubious status of a word. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i "Don't visit him," she said.\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100 Single quotation marks (' ') are used most frequently for quotes within quotes. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100\i Marie told the teacher, "I saw Marc at the playground, and he said to me 'Bill started the fight,' and I believed him."\i0  \par

\pard\sb100\sa100\b Ellipsis(. . .)      :\b0   Ellipsis is used in writing or printing to indicate an omission, especially of letters or words. Ellipses are frequently used within quotations to jump from one phrase to another, omitting unneeded words that do not interfere with the meaning. Writing research papers or newspapers quoting parts of speeches will often employ ellipsis to avoid copying lengthy text that is not needed. \par

\pard{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}{\*\pn\pnlvlblt\pnf3\pnindent360{\pntxtb\'B7}}\fi-360\li720\sb100\sa100 Omission of words: \i She began to count, "One, two, three, four\'85" until she got to 10, then went to find him.\i0  \par
{\pntext\f3\'B7\tab}Within a quotation: \i When Newton stated, "An object at rest stays at rest and an object in motion stays in motion..." he developed the law of motion.\i0  \par

